<p>MySQL is the one adapter whose connection failure isn't a Pop exception. PHP's mysqli driver throws
<code>mysqli_sql_exception</code> inside <code>new \mysqli(...)</code>, before the adapter inspects <code>connect_error</code>, so catch that
as well.</p>
<h2 id="postgresql">PostgreSQL<a class="anchor" href="#postgresql" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>

<p><code>Pop\Db\Adapter\Pgsql</code> wraps the <code>pg_*</code> functions from the <code>pgsql</code> extension. Options beyond the four
basics are appended to the connection string as-is.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Option</th>
<th></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>database</code></td>
<td>required</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>username</code></td>
<td>required</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>password</code></td>
<td>required</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>host</code></td>
<td>optional, defaults to <code>localhost</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>port</code>, <code>hostaddr</code>, <code>connect_timeout</code>, <code>options</code>, <code>sslmode</code></td>
<td>optional, each appended to the connection string</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>persist</code></td>
<td>truthy to connect through <code>pg_pconnect()</code> instead of <code>pg_connect()</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
